The prime \(p\) must satisfy \(n^4 \equiv -1 \pmod{p^2}\) for some positive integer \(n\). No such prime exists for \(p = 2\) or \(p = 3\), as fourth-power residues modulo \(4\) and \(9\) never include \(-1\). For any odd prime \(p \equiv 1 \pmod{8}\), the congruence \(x^4 \equiv -1 \pmod{p}\) has solutions (the multiplicative group modulo \(p\) contains an element of order \(8\)) whose derivatives under \(x^4 + 1\) are nonzero, so Hensel's lemma guarantees unique lifts to solutions modulo \(p^2\). The smallest such prime is therefore \(p = 17\).

The four solutions modulo \(17\) are \(x \equiv 2, 8, 9, 15\). Each lifts by the substitution \(x = a + 17k\) and reduction of the resulting binomial expansion modulo \(289\):
- \(a = 2\) yields \(k \equiv 9 \pmod{17}\), hence \(x \equiv 155 \pmod{289}\);
